@startuml
title IPlayer — who makes the decisions taken during resolution

skinparam linetype ortho

/'
  Several things cannot be decided when a card is constructed, only when it
  resolves: ChoiceAction picks a branch, ChosenFrom picks a bench slot, a
  knockout forces a replacement active. All of them route through here.
'/

interface IPlayer {
    + name(): String
    + choose(Decision<T>): T
}

class "Decision<T>" as Decision <<record>> {
    - prompt: String
    - options: List<T>
    /' Not always the controller — plenty of cards make the OPPONENT choose. '/
    - chooser: Side
    - context: ResolutionContext
}

/' Picks uniformly from a seeded RandomSource. Self-play, fuzzing, and a
   baseline to measure any real AI against. '/
class RandomPlayer implements IPlayer {
    - rng: RandomSource
}

/' Replays a fixed list of answers, failing loudly if the game asks a question
   the script did not anticipate. This is what makes rule tests deterministic
   and readable. '/
class ScriptedPlayer implements IPlayer {
    - answers: Queue<Integer>
}

class ConsolePlayer implements IPlayer {}

IPlayer ..> Decision
Decision o-- Side
Decision o-- ResolutionContext

note bottom of IPlayer
  One generic `choose` rather than a method per decision kind
  (chooseBenchSlot, chooseDiscard, chooseAttack, ...): the engine
  always presents a list of legal options and receives one back,
  so adding a new kind of decision never widens the interface.

  Battle.legalActions(Side) uses the same shape for the turn
  itself — a turn is just Decision<IAction>. See GameFlow.puml.
end note

@enduml
